00:12:03.679 --> 00:12:06.966
could you tell us about the executive
challenge, first to your students,

00:12:06.966 --> 00:12:07.761
parts eight and.

00:12:07.761 --> 00:12:11.348
Well, I don't have much to say except
that everyone is so dressed out today.

00:12:11.348 --> 00:12:13.980
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; One of the few days you'll see

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:16.280
everyone at the GSP wearing a suit.

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:17.110
&gt;&gt; Suits, yeah.

00:12:17.110 --> 00:12:20.760
&gt;&gt; But it's a really great just
bonding experience with your squad.

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:23.620
So your squad is six other MBA ones and

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:28.120
an MBA two facilitator that you
do this whole first quarter

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:32.410
kind of experience with and then it kind
of culminates in this executive challenge.

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:34.730
Where you get a case and you go up, and

00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:39.720
you kind of do a mock role play with
really successful executive alums.

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:45.130
And it's just a really good way to kind of
put all that you've learned first quarter

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:46.540
into this experience.

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:50.670
And I just, what I remember most is
just really bonding with my squad and

00:12:50.670 --> 00:12:53.450
having a fun day to kind of
culminate then to fall quarter.

00:12:53.450 --> 00:12:55.860
&gt;&gt; And like do some role plays.

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:59.380
It's like you give us kisses and
we do the road plays as

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:03.450
if we were executives now and
then towards the end once squad wins.

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:04.805
But there's no price.

00:13:04.805 --> 00:13:06.300
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's like a fun day.

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:09.390
That is amazing.

00:13:09.390 --> 00:13:15.710
Could you comment on how the Bay Area
influences your MBA experience?

00:13:15.710 --> 00:13:19.990
I think as someone interested in tech,
this has been very interesting for

00:13:19.990 --> 00:13:23.910
me, A,
in picking which school I wanted to go to.

00:13:23.910 --> 00:13:28.740
But B, I think just the fact that we
breathe the same air of the combinely

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:32.760
makes it sound like I mean, you just
cannot not know so much about that

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:36.480
combinely and what's happening here and
like the tech landscape and all of that.

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:39.030
And I think that's really,
really important

00:13:39.030 --> 00:13:42.830
as someone who wanted to work in tech,
but I would say even if you're not.

00:13:42.830 --> 00:13:45.370
And I would be curious
to hear your opinion,

00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:48.080
since you are not interested in tech,
obviously.

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:50.610
I feel like there are.

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:53.380
There's access to all
the other industries as well.

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:54.730
And I don't know.

00:13:54.730 --> 00:13:57.230
Can you speak more if you
are interested in finance or

00:13:57.230 --> 00:13:58.160
consulting or
&gt;&gt; [COUGH]

00:13:58.160 --> 00:13:59.030
&gt;&gt; Other jobs.

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:00.700
How does that influence your experience?

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:01.360
&gt;&gt; Definitely.
So

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:05.220
I can speak of someone that techs
great but I don't think it's for me.

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:10.210
And I'm entrepreneurship curious but I'm
not all in like a lot of our class mates.

00:14:10.210 --> 00:14:12.000
But I still love being in the bay.

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:15.260
One, just to access to the people
that are around you are phenomenal.

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:19.090
We just get fantastic speakers,
if they're not already in the bay,

00:14:19.090 --> 00:14:22.320
they cycle through and
visit, so that's been great.

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:25.670
You just got to visit very easily
all these awesome companies.

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:30.200
I've gone to Facebook several times,
Google, Uber, Waymo,

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:35.320
these are all like ten, 15 minute ride
away and that's been really cool.

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:38.880
And I just feel like there's
entrepreneurial energy at the bay.

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:41.920
And everyone is really trying to
create something or grow something or

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:43.020
change something.

00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:47.420
And that definitely is the Stanford
ecosystem goes well with that.

00:14:47.420 --> 00:14:52.555
&gt;&gt; Yeah and I remember we both actually
organized the woman and management trick.

00:14:52.555 --> 00:14:54.560
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; To the point there's so

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:57.420
many companies and
I don't know ten or 15 minute drive.

00:14:57.420 --> 00:14:59.094
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's cool.

00:14:59.094 --> 00:15:03.402
&gt;&gt; It was amazing to meet amazing
women from all these companies and

00:15:03.402 --> 00:15:05.810
just hear about their experience.

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:11.140
So I think easy access is one of
the biggest things that we get at the GSP.

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:12.070
Awesome.

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:17.360
Hannah, what were your career goals before
you started GSP, and have they changed?

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:19.060
&gt;&gt; Definitely so my career.

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:20.750
I do have to get back to San Diego.

00:15:20.750 --> 00:15:23.710
My partner's still down
there with the navy so

00:15:23.710 --> 00:15:26.520
consulting made sense because
it allows me to do that.

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:29.460
But I wasn't dead set
when I came to the GSP I

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:31.680
explored a couple of different things.

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:35.530
I'm still like maybe I should do something
entrepreneurial next quarter on the side.

00:15:35.530 --> 00:15:37.710
Sides, so I haven't closed that off.

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:41.570
But overall, I decided that just
consulting was the right fit, allows me to

00:15:41.570 --> 00:15:46.180
kind of get that analytics skill set that
a lot of my classmates already have.

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:50.009
And really kind of get that first glimpse
at the private sector before kind of

00:15:50.009 --> 00:15:51.904
thinking what I want to do elsewhere.

00:15:51.904 --> 00:15:58.689
&gt;&gt; Awesome, yeah, and I think Not similar
to you, actually opposite to you.

00:15:58.689 --> 00:16:00.740
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I did not have any curriculars before

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:01.498
going to the GSB.

00:16:01.498 --> 00:16:04.284
I have been in tech for like all my life.

00:16:04.284 --> 00:16:06.307
And so I was like,
I'm not going to do tech.

00:16:06.307 --> 00:16:10.870
But once I came to the GSB,
I actually realized that I like tech.

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:14.660
I like it more than anything
else that was offered, and

00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:19.300
I think that was when I realized that I
wanted to do it even after graduation.

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:22.830
I shifted more towards tech in media
because I figured that's the space that

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:23.390
I wanted.

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:28.340
But I really appreciated the chance to get
to know yourself more and realize more

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:31.790
what are the things that you like and
what are the things that you don't.

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:36.503
And I think sometimes reaffirming the
things that we thought we did not like is

00:16:36.503 --> 00:16:39.330
also a good outcome of the MBA.

00:16:39.330 --> 00:16:44.399
So yeah, I am just going back to tech, but
I am going back way more prepared, I hope.

00:16:45.940 --> 00:16:48.931
And you interned at SAP this summer,
which is pretty cool.

00:16:48.931 --> 00:16:50.282
&gt;&gt; My god, it's dope.

00:16:50.282 --> 00:16:57.831
[LAUGH] Awesome, so we got another
question about leadership labs.

00:16:57.831 --> 00:17:02.428
Can you describe your experience
with leadership labs and

00:17:02.428 --> 00:17:05.063
what you learned most from them?

00:17:05.063 --> 00:17:09.052
&gt;&gt; I think what I learned most from lead
labs was I was used to kind of interacting

00:17:09.052 --> 00:17:11.029
with very specific types of people.

00:17:11.029 --> 00:17:15.100
Like military style of leadership is
very different than the private sector.

00:17:15.100 --> 00:17:18.200
So I think I learned a lot
more of those soft skills and

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:22.520
how to come off softer in less direct and
have more influence.

00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:26.510
And you get pretty close with your squad
and they're very comfortable giving you

00:17:26.510 --> 00:17:31.280
honest feedback in how you come off when
you are kind of leading a management.

00:17:31.280 --> 00:17:35.170
So I think of anything it was just good,
honest feedback and

00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:38.330
a very friendly warm environment.

00:17:38.330 --> 00:17:42.898
And then just also kind of in a squad in
a smaller team you see what other people's

00:17:42.898 --> 00:17:45.980
strengths are and
you kind of learn from them as well.

00:17:45.980 --> 00:17:47.650
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah, totally.

00:17:47.650 --> 00:17:49.474
To me as well, I think it's feedback.

00:17:49.474 --> 00:17:53.657
As you mentioned, we keep saying at
the GSB, that feedback is a gift.

00:17:53.657 --> 00:17:54.630
&gt;&gt; I love to say that.

00:17:54.630 --> 00:17:58.900
And I think just getting continuous
feedback because with leadership labs,

00:17:58.900 --> 00:18:04.330
you meet every Wednesday to do role plays,
and you get feedback on how those went.

00:18:04.330 --> 00:18:07.450
So I think getting continuous
feedback allows you to see those

00:18:07.450 --> 00:18:09.720
blind spots that you didn't
know about yourself before.

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:17.316
And I think that has been my
biggest learning from lead labs.

00:18:17.316 --> 00:18:20.210
Awesome, I'll take this one,
I'll give you more time to think about it.

00:18:20.210 --> 00:18:22.630
If you were to go back in time and

00:18:22.630 --> 00:18:25.700
do something differently at GSB,
what would it be?

00:18:28.210 --> 00:18:30.188
Well, it's hard, because I see now,

00:18:30.188 --> 00:18:33.400
we already are,
we know what we've been through.

00:18:33.400 --> 00:18:37.898
And so it's so easy to say,
I would be less hard on myself.

00:18:37.898 --> 00:18:41.470
But I don't think that that would've
been the case if I started again.

00:18:41.470 --> 00:18:47.070
I think, when you come in to a place
where people are super accomplished, and

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:52.174
smart, and everyone is just amazing,
you start doubting yourself.

00:18:52.174 --> 00:18:54.918
And that could cause you to become
really really hard on yourself,

00:18:54.918 --> 00:18:56.160
at least in my case.

00:18:56.160 --> 00:19:00.760
But things ended up to be really
really fine and I'm good.

00:19:00.760 --> 00:19:02.410
I'm in my second year.

00:19:02.410 --> 00:19:04.250
I'm interviewing for jobs.

00:19:04.250 --> 00:19:05.690
Everything's super fine.

00:19:05.690 --> 00:19:09.670
So I wish that I had
this mindset coming in.

00:19:09.670 --> 00:19:13.440
But I know that it's hard, but
this has been just my own experience.

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:15.189
Would you do anything differently, Hannah?

00:19:15.189 --> 00:19:17.738
&gt;&gt; Yes,
this was a very easy one to answer.

00:19:17.738 --> 00:19:21.830
I think I overcommitted on some
extracurriculars year one.

00:19:21.830 --> 00:19:25.420
And the issue with that is then you
don't have the bandwidth or capacity for

00:19:25.420 --> 00:19:27.430
extracurriculars later on.

00:19:27.430 --> 00:19:30.740
So you get to the GSB and there are all
these kind of shiny objects and

00:19:30.740 --> 00:19:31.980
you want to do it all.

00:19:31.980 --> 00:19:34.450
And I think I said yes
to too many of them.

00:19:34.450 --> 00:19:37.310
And now second year,
there are these great opportunities.

00:19:37.310 --> 00:19:40.600
I'm like, I don't really have the time or
capacity for that.

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:41.921
So looking back year one,

00:19:41.921 --> 00:19:45.474
I would have really thought about
each thing before I committed to it.

00:19:45.474 --> 00:19:49.630
And really been kind of ruthlessly
prioritizing those things.

00:19:49.630 --> 00:19:51.495
And then just really asking myself,

00:19:51.495 --> 00:19:54.450
what will I get out of this
extracurricular involvement?

00:19:54.450 --> 00:19:58.230
So overall, being president of
the Vets Club has been great.

00:19:58.230 --> 00:20:00.810
Leading the Japan global study trip,
that's going to be great.

00:20:00.810 --> 00:20:02.120
But there are a couple other ones,

00:20:02.120 --> 00:20:04.910
I'm like,
is this really worth the time commitment?

00:20:04.910 --> 00:20:07.310
&gt;&gt; Awesome, well,
this is great that you mentioned that.

00:20:07.310 --> 00:20:10.800
because we have a question actually
about the extracurricular activities.

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:14.420
So can you tell us what are some of
the most popular extracurricular

00:20:14.420 --> 00:20:16.260
activities here at the GSB?

00:20:16.260 --> 00:20:18.440
Maybe to the lens of what you've done, but

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:21.480
also other things that are popular
that you haven't done.

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:25.264
&gt;&gt; Definitely, I think so
our vocal fellows, which we're both not.

00:20:25.264 --> 00:20:26.284
&gt;&gt; We're both not.

00:20:26.284 --> 00:20:30.111
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] That's one of the most famous
kind of extracurricular things you can do.

00:20:30.111 --> 00:20:34.562
You'll take touchy feely as a first year
and then second year, you'll be that MBA

00:20:34.562 --> 00:20:39.150
to squad facilitator, which they're
all right now at executive challenge.

00:20:39.150 --> 00:20:43.440
So that's a pretty kind of classic,
major time commitment, extracurricular.

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:47.960
Otherwise, there's a ton of different
clubs and clubs are like affinity groups,

00:20:47.960 --> 00:20:52.530
and then clubs are professional kind of
interest and then there are just kind of

00:20:52.530 --> 00:20:57.530
social, wine club, or eat club,
those kind of more fun interests.

00:20:57.530 --> 00:21:01.890
So a lot of people join one of those and
take a leadership position in those clubs.

00:21:01.890 --> 00:21:05.530
I mentioned, I'm president of
Veterans Club, that's been great.

00:21:05.530 --> 00:21:08.458
People also TA,
I'm TA-ing next quarter for

00:21:08.458 --> 00:21:13.127
two real estate lectures that are very
successful and really great guys.

00:21:13.127 --> 00:21:14.124
&gt;&gt; Lucky for you.

00:21:14.124 --> 00:21:17.364
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I know you're-
&gt;&gt; I already TAed this course, but yeah.

00:21:17.364 --> 00:21:22.322
&gt;&gt; Right, and then people also work at
maybe like a venture capital firm that has

00:21:22.322 --> 00:21:24.570
a relationship with the GSB.

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:28.420
Tons of people are starting their own
companies or working with a startup.

00:21:28.420 --> 00:21:32.055
So there's so
much extracurricular stuff going on and

00:21:32.055 --> 00:21:34.512
it's hard to kind of mention it all.

00:21:34.512 --> 00:21:38.172
&gt;&gt; Totally, and to your point even though
our vocal is like the most pure or

00:21:38.172 --> 00:21:41.108
something, I feel like I have done so
many other things.

00:21:41.108 --> 00:21:44.458
And both of us have done so
many things that are not as popular.

00:21:44.458 --> 00:21:48.807
But I feel that they spoke to us more
maybe, to your point it's clubs or

00:21:48.807 --> 00:21:53.923
things that you just enjoy doing like
TA-ing that course or something like that.

00:21:53.923 --> 00:21:57.092
I also love doing work with the MBA
office and just the admissions and

00:21:57.092 --> 00:21:58.101
speaking to admins.

00:21:58.101 --> 00:22:01.847
I think that's something
I really enjoy doing.

00:22:01.847 --> 00:22:06.632
I also was on the Distinguished
Teaching Award Committee,

00:22:06.632 --> 00:22:12.479
where we get to pick a Stanford professor
that we want to give an award to.

00:22:12.479 --> 00:22:14.204
That's been extra fun for me.

00:22:14.204 --> 00:22:18.894
So, yeah, I will just say that even if you
don't get into one of the most popular

00:22:18.894 --> 00:22:23.662
extracurricular activities for whatever
reason, there's just so much to do.

00:22:23.662 --> 00:22:28.399
And I love the sheet that we have you get
when you get in of all the extracurricular

00:22:28.399 --> 00:22:32.233
activities and how much time
commitment each of them requires.

00:22:32.233 --> 00:22:35.818
So that you can plan to avoid
over-committing as you mentioned.

00:22:35.818 --> 00:22:36.676
&gt;&gt; But you won't avoid it.

00:22:36.676 --> 00:22:40.909
&gt;&gt; You will overcome it,
it's just like, awesome.

00:22:40.909 --> 00:22:45.821
What is the most valuable
thing you have learned so far?

00:22:45.821 --> 00:22:46.827
This is deep.

00:22:46.827 --> 00:22:48.649
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My God, I guess.

00:22:48.649 --> 00:22:53.677
&gt;&gt; This one-
&gt;&gt; I think maybe through talk.

00:22:53.677 --> 00:22:54.869
And we mentioned talk,

00:22:54.869 --> 00:22:58.563
that's where once a week to students
spend about 30 minutes kind of like

00:22:58.563 --> 00:23:03.020
telling their life story and major events
or people that have impacted them.

00:23:03.020 --> 00:23:06.650
I think from that I just learned,
you really can't judge people or

00:23:06.650 --> 00:23:09.530
know what they're going through or
where they've come from.

00:23:09.530 --> 00:23:12.280
And like you think you know someone and
then you hear their story and

00:23:12.280 --> 00:23:14.350
you're like, I had no idea.

00:23:14.350 --> 00:23:18.986
That's just been like, been kind of
like a fantastic weekly reminder of

00:23:18.986 --> 00:23:23.546
the importance of you don't truly
know someone and showing empathy and

00:23:23.546 --> 00:23:26.592
just wanting to hear
other people's stories.

00:23:26.592 --> 00:23:28.274
I don't know that's the most but

00:23:28.274 --> 00:23:31.467
that's just something I look
forward to on a on a weekly basis.

00:23:31.467 --> 00:23:33.790
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah, that's interesting.

00:23:33.790 --> 00:23:37.020
And I think the other thing
that was very valuable for

00:23:37.020 --> 00:23:39.749
me is learning about myself more, as well.

00:23:39.749 --> 00:23:44.025
I think that we do a lot of sort of
introspective exercises at the GSB.

00:23:44.025 --> 00:23:48.921
And we spend a lot of time with ourselves
and journal and all of that in so many

00:23:48.921 --> 00:23:54.469
courses that I think you just become deep
with you emotions and more introspective.

00:23:54.469 --> 00:23:57.295
And I think just learning more about
yourself has been a very valuable

00:23:57.295 --> 00:23:57.890
thing for me.

00:23:59.970 --> 00:24:03.936
What are you looking forward
to the most next quarter?

00:24:03.936 --> 00:24:06.867
My fiscal policy course,
[LAUGH] with Keith Hennessy.

00:24:06.867 --> 00:24:11.468
because I just like it.
&gt;&gt; I took that one, it's really good.

00:24:11.468 --> 00:24:12.629
&gt;&gt; You took?
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:24:12.629 --> 00:24:15.490
&gt;&gt; You took all the courses that I like.

00:24:15.490 --> 00:24:19.953
I think it's this and also low key
notes are coming next quarters.

00:24:19.953 --> 00:24:24.625
So I am super looking forward to either
delivering one or even getting to know

00:24:24.625 --> 00:24:28.718
the stories, like ideas to change
the world from our classmates.

00:24:28.718 --> 00:24:32.567
&gt;&gt; Low key notes are like these kind of
ten minute TED talks that students give

00:24:32.567 --> 00:24:33.946
and they are really cool.

00:24:33.946 --> 00:24:35.275
But you're giving one the next quarter.

00:24:35.275 --> 00:24:38.021
&gt;&gt; I hope so, the results
are still not out so we'll see.

00:24:38.021 --> 00:24:39.255
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's awesome.

00:24:39.255 --> 00:24:40.729
&gt;&gt; Are you looking forward to any?

00:24:40.729 --> 00:24:44.325
&gt;&gt; Next quarter, so
that's when I'll be TA-ing and

00:24:44.325 --> 00:24:48.600
planning our kind of big military
service community dinner.

00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:52.568
I just learned that we got General Mattis,
former Secretary of Defense,

00:24:52.568 --> 00:24:53.921
as our keynote speaker.

00:24:53.921 --> 00:24:57.442
Which is pretty crazy to be
emailing with General Mattis.

00:24:57.442 --> 00:25:01.203
And then also our Japan
trip's in the spring, so

00:25:01.203 --> 00:25:04.980
kind of the last minute or
the last preps for that.

00:25:04.980 --> 00:25:08.350
So that's kind of like when all my
extracurriculars will come to a head and

00:25:08.350 --> 00:25:11.510
looking forward to like really
seeing the fruits of my labor.

00:25:11.510 --> 00:25:15.166
Other than that, it's a good time just to
go snowboarding, easy to get to Tahoe.

00:25:15.166 --> 00:25:19.970
And as an avid snowboarder,
I'm also looking forward to that.

00:25:19.970 --> 00:25:21.944
&gt;&gt; Yeah, also-
&gt;&gt; And.

00:25:21.944 --> 00:25:25.375
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
[LAUGH] I only have around five minutes

00:25:25.375 --> 00:25:26.450
left.

00:25:26.450 --> 00:25:29.535
We've received so
many questions on the application and

00:25:29.535 --> 00:25:33.465
again I would sort of ask people to
send the admission office about them.

00:25:33.465 --> 00:25:37.080
But maybe we can generally
just take one question.

00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:39.227
Could you describe your
application experience and

00:25:39.227 --> 00:25:41.430
what would you do differently
now that you look back?

00:25:41.430 --> 00:25:47.470
I think just like a general piece of
advice to all the prospective students.

00:25:47.470 --> 00:25:49.839
All right that if I
were to think about it,

00:25:49.839 --> 00:25:52.428
I would not have changed
anything that I did.

00:25:52.428 --> 00:25:56.530
I would probably just be less
stressed out about the whole thing.

00:25:56.530 --> 00:25:59.990
Honestly, what the GSB is interested in
is just, as we mentioned, knowing you.

00:25:59.990 --> 00:26:04.301
So how can you present your story and
what can you tell and

00:26:04.301 --> 00:26:08.537
show to people in the application
is all that matters.

00:26:08.537 --> 00:26:12.171
I wouldn't worry about like getting
references from, I don't know,

00:26:12.171 --> 00:26:16.160
people that are really famous in
the business world or something like that.

00:26:16.160 --> 00:26:20.180
I didn't have any of these two
Jordanian people recommended me so

00:26:20.180 --> 00:26:21.680
that's absolutely fine.

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:25.290
I would really focus on telling my story,
and yeah that's it.

00:26:25.290 --> 00:26:26.560
